  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nGlobal guidance brings the upper level low which is currently near \nthe New Mexico/Arizona border northward around the upper level high \nand then into the central Plains Thursday night.  The wave is picked \nup in the mean northwest flow aloft and it moves southeast into the \nMississippi Valley by late Friday.  Models are showing some weak to \nmoderate lo level cyclogenesis over the central Plains in response \nto the upper forcing.  The gradient between the high over the Ohio \nValley and the developing low over the Plains produces a 30-40kt low \nlevel jet late Thursday night that shifts into the lower Missouri \nand Mid Mississippi Valley Friday.  This results in what is likely \nbe the best chance for precipitation for our area over the next 7 \ndays.  The highest chance for rain remains along and west of the \nMississippi as high pressure to the east will continue to funnel \ndrier air into the region which undercuts the moisture convergence \nproduced by the low level jet.  Clouds and precip also limit daytime \nheating on Friday with most locations struggling to reach the the \nmid and upper 70s.\n\nThe short wave continues to move southeast away from the area Friday \nnight, and the upper ridge builds east into the Mississippi Valley \nSaturday.  This will bring warmer temperatures and higher humidity \nback to Missouri and Illinois for the weekend, although there's some \nquestion as to how fast the warmup will occur.  A little over 60% of \nthe LREF members keep max temperatures below 90 on Saturday. However \nsome of the warmer 40% are very warm with highs over 95.  These are \nthe outliers...though the operational GFS is among them with 850mb \ntemps around 22C which mixes down to 95F at the surface.  NBM temps \nin the mid 80s to around 90 look reasonable given the uncertainty \nfor Saturday's highs.  The upper ridge continues building eastward \nthrough the weekend into next week, although deterministic guidance \nshows another weak short wave moving across the Mississippi Valley \nthrough Iowa into central Illinois.  This attenuates the ridge \nsomewhat, but it doesn't look strong enough to spare our area from \nanother period of mid 90s temperatures along with low to mid 70s dew \npoints.  This would put us squarely back into Heat Advisory \nterritory for most of the area for at least Sunday and Monday if \nthese conditions are realized.",
